WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.550
Film Courage: When I go to your website for
THEN THERE WAS JOE, I notice you have a ton

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:11.180
of press from your home town or surrounding
areas and they seem so welcoming.

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:18.180
How is that to know that yes when you come
to your new home in LA it’s a different

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:24.670
ballgame, it’s not personal but there are
just so many people doing the same thing.

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:30.831
To be so embraced and cheered on and then
coming back here knowing that you have to

00:00:30.831 --> 00:00:31.980
be your own cheerleader.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:38.910
Justin Warren, Writer/Director: So what’s
interesting about (and this is kind of my

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:44.070
strategy actually for making my first film)
I wanted to do it in a place where I’d be

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:54.660
supported and because the crazy part is that
anywhere outside of LA outside of California,

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:59.760
if you make a movie that’s newsworthy because
it’s just not a normal thing that a lot

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:06.950
of people do but out here in LA everybody
is making a movie.

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:16.010
And I hate to use the term everybody but a
whole lot of people are making stuff to the

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:21.010
point where it’s relatively normalized.

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:29.610
Like for instance whenever I go to a coffee
shop, if you just look at people’s laptops,

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:32.750
9-out-of-10 screens some one is writing a
screenplay.

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:39.620
And that is not normal anywhere else outside
of LA.

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:45.820
It was amazing, we premiered my film THEN
THERE WAS JOE with the Arkansas Cinema Society

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:50.370
which is run by Jeff Nichols who directed
MUDD and MIDNIGHT SPECIAL.

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:59.700
I was great because they saw my film and believed
in it and were going to put it on a premiere

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:01.710
for it.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:09.360
There was just a level of excitement that
I just haven’t experienced anywhere else

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:16.520
because it was a bit of a hometown hero kind
of vibe but also people just truly went to

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:18.270
the movie to enjoy themselves.

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:24.060
I think sometimes here in LA we can go to
the movies to judge, that’s why we go.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:28.500
We walk in and are like “This better impress
me.”

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:36.550
Back home in Arkansas it’s almost like a
magic you walk into this dark room with other

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:40.450
strangers and it’s like “Okay, what’s
going to happen.”

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:46.190
And being in that posture of about to watch
a film is a totally different vibe.

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:51.340
And I think that actually most of the world
experiences movies that way.

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:57.240
Of I want to go and I want to be entertained,
I want to be moved and I want to have fun.

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:02.330
And sometimes living out here in LA it can
be easy to lose track of that because we try

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:07.569
to impress our filmmaker friends “Did you
see that dope shot on that thing?

00:03:07.569 --> 00:03:09.300
It was tight right?”

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:15.940
I knew that the thing I was trying to make
is for an audience to consume.

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:23.739
It’s for people who live lives outside of
the entertainment industry.

00:03:23.739 --> 00:03:28.850
It’s about regular people going through
what they’re doing and there’s reception

00:03:28.850 --> 00:03:30.170
at the end.

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:37.070
And I know sometimes when I’m shooting my
scripts to friends or they shoot my their

00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:44.819
scripts I think it was like George Lucas or
something who said “There are only like

00:03:44.819 --> 00:03:47.110
seven plots.”

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:50.550
And there is some truth to that I think.

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:56.670
But just like you can only make car one way
really.

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:58.460
It usually have four wheels.

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:02.550
It sometimes doesn’t have to have four wheels,
you can make it with three wheels.

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:13.680
But the reality is that I think the majority
of people will usually give a movie a chance

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:18.029
in ways that sometimes after you’ve gone
through film school you’ve already decided

00:04:18.029 --> 00:04:22.529
“I’ve already seen this before because
I know where this is going.”

00:04:22.529 --> 00:04:24.639
And you’re way ahead of the movie.

00:04:24.639 --> 00:04:29.379
And you can get into that habit and stop enjoying
films.

00:04:29.379 --> 00:04:36.020
And so what making my feature taught me is
that it helped me remember what movies are

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:43.849
for, what they do and what they mean to me
and seeing that in Arkansas was just a beautiful,

00:04:43.849 --> 00:04:48.020
beautiful experience and rejuvenated me and
made me want to keep going because I got really

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:53.650
scared after I finished my film because I
was so tired, I was so exhausted.

00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:56.689
I had a moment where I was like “I don’t
think I’m going to do this anymore.

00:04:56.689 --> 00:04:59.600
I think I might be one and done.”

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:07.789
And something happened when the lights went
down and everybody got quiet and my movie

00:05:07.789 --> 00:05:11.639
is a comedy drama, it’s a dramedy if you
will.

00:05:11.639 --> 00:05:17.419
But the first couple title cards it’s a
joke and it takes you a second to get it because

00:05:17.419 --> 00:05:21.809
it starts and then there’s another title
card and then another and then another.

00:05:21.809 --> 00:05:28.099
And everybody immediately busted out laughing
and I’m like “Ahhh, they’re in.

00:05:28.099 --> 00:05:29.099
They get it.

00:05:29.099 --> 00:05:30.169
They get it.”

00:05:30.169 --> 00:05:34.292
And I showed that to a lot of my friends out
here and they were like “This is dumb.

00:05:34.292 --> 00:05:38.120
This is really dumb.”

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:40.189
But I knew it was funny.

00:05:40.189 --> 00:05:47.439
That’s another thing it’s taught me (this
whole process) it’s like trusting your voice

00:05:47.439 --> 00:05:54.699
and knowing that your voice and your opinion
about something is totally valid.

00:05:54.699 --> 00:06:01.210
And even if it’s standing outside of a group
of people and a group of people don’t agree,

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:05.800
if you feel strongly about something, there’s
probably at least a million other people in

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:08.169
the world that feel the same way.

00:06:08.169 --> 00:06:16.990
And this process (the notes process) we were
talking about earlier helped me to know when

00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:18.300
to trust my voice.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:22.490
Film Courage: I think you said your family
has this unofficial rule?

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:26.430
I saw this in one of the interviews that you
did “If there is something you’re afraid

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:33.389
of that will make you a stronger, better person,
that’s probably the thing you need to do.”

00:06:33.389 --> 00:06:34.389
That’s great.

00:06:34.389 --> 00:06:37.559
Justin: Yes because I think fear is probably
a really good indicator.

00:06:37.559 --> 00:06:44.309
It’s an indicator of your destiny in a really
weird way.

00:06:44.309 --> 00:06:46.559
I don’t want to get too new-agey in here.

00:06:46.559 --> 00:06:49.879
Film Courage: Hang on, let me turn off the
incense.

00:06:49.879 --> 00:06:56.529
Justin: But to me in my brain I feel like
there’s a better side of you on the other

00:06:56.529 --> 00:06:58.449
side of fear.

00:06:58.449 --> 00:07:03.949
Because things I’m not afraid of are things
that don’t really mean that much to me.

00:07:03.949 --> 00:07:08.969
I’m usually afraid of the things that mean
the most to me.

00:07:08.969 --> 00:07:14.330
Right before we launched the Kickstarter campaign
for my film, I had never experienced that

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:18.949
much fear in my whole life because I was like
“Oh my Gosh, this is public.

00:07:18.949 --> 00:07:20.849
If I fail it’s going to be public.

00:07:20.849 --> 00:07:22.990
Everyone is going to know about it.”

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:31.860
And so there was like a week where I didn’t
post it and so I was like “I don’t know

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:33.559
if I can do this right now.”

00:07:33.559 --> 00:07:38.139
And so thankfully I have a really supportive
family and a really supportive wife and they

00:07:38.139 --> 00:07:40.169
were like “You have to do this.

00:07:40.169 --> 00:07:41.169
You have to.

00:07:41.169 --> 00:07:45.169
It’s your destiny.”

00:07:45.169 --> 00:07:50.129
And I’ve noticed that every single time
where I’ve been utterly terrified and I

00:07:50.129 --> 00:07:54.060
pushed through it, I’ve only succeeded.

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:58.219
And I don’t mean that in like an arrogant
way, I mean that in the sense of like I jumped

00:07:58.219 --> 00:08:03.719
off the cliff and suddenly a trampoline appeared
right before I smacked the ground.

00:08:03.719 --> 00:08:08.199
And that has happened so many times like for
anyone who has put together a feature film,

00:08:08.199 --> 00:08:11.289
you understand that.

00:08:11.289 --> 00:08:14.490
You know that those things happen.

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:19.080
You kind of move before any of the pieces
are in place and suddenly magically the pieces

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:20.719
are in place.

00:08:20.719 --> 00:08:29.909
And I now love that feeling, sort of jumping
off to see “Okay, is something going to

00:08:29.909 --> 00:08:31.550
catch me.”

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:39.040
And nine times out of ten it does and when
it doesn’t I’ll figure it out.

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:45.810
And that’s been a recurring theme throughout
my whole life.

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:46.990
If you’re afraid of it you have to do it.

